bibliograph (0.1.11) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Point both Constellation backlink reads at the field the records actually
    carry. A `source` is `<collection>:<field path>`, and both `bookShelving`
    and `bookReview` hold the book as the strongRef the lexicons declare —
    `book: { uri, cid }` — so the path is `book.uri`. The shelving read asked
    for `book.ref`, a key no record has, which left `getShelvingOfBook`'s
    discovery tier unable to match anything while its database tier read
    `records.value->'book'->>'uri'`; the two halves of the same merge
    disagreed about the shape. Reviews had the same fault one level up: the
    edition feed asked for `bookReview:book` and matched nothing, so the
    review side of that feed returned empty for every edition.
  * Type the two filters as `ShelvingSource` / `ReviewSource` unions, which is
    what let the wrong path stay invisible: the strings were untyped at both
    call sites. The shelving hydration tests now name the field they build.
  * No behaviour change for records already indexed correctly, and no
    migration: the index is rebuilt by the upstream Constellation instance.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 00:23:14 -0500

bibliograph (0.1.10) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Cache Google Books responses in Postgres for 24 hours. The GB quota is
    charged per request against one API key that the web server and both
    workers share, and the same questions get asked over and over: the search
    box query that was just run, the shelf hydration of the same volume id,
    and — far more than either — the same `q=isbn:` lookup for every edition an
    ISBN backfill touches. Every GB call already funnelled through `gbFetch`,
    so the cache lives there: one entry serves every process and survives a
    restart or a deploy, which a process-local Map (as used for Wikidata)
    cannot.
  * Cache misses as well as hits. The quota is spent by asking, not by the
    answer, so a remembered "no such volume" (the 404 from `volumes/{id}`)
    saves exactly as much as a hit and is stored with the same 24-hour TTL; a
    NULL body is what distinguishes it from "nothing cached". Transient
    outcomes — 429, 5xx, timeouts, aborts — are deliberately never stored, or
    one bad minute upstream would be replayed all day.
  * Route ISBN enrichment through `gbFetch`. `enrichEditions` was the only GB
    caller still using raw `fetch`, which left it the one path the cache would
    not have covered — and it is the heaviest consumer of the quota, running
    eight lookups at a time across a whole backfill page. It now shares the
    cache, retry, breaker and metric accounting every other GB call has.
  * Key entries on the request URL with the API key dropped and the parameters
    sorted, so key rotation, keyless volume lookups and an equivalent query
    built in a different order all reuse one entry, and the credential is never
    written to the database. Expired rows are swept in bounded batches by the
    cache writer, because the table has no other reaper.
  * Add `google_books_cache_total{result}`: `hit` and `negative` are requests
    served without touching upstream, `store` counts the writes that make that
    possible, and `error` is a cache failure that was swallowed so the request
    fell through to upstream. A broken cache degrades to the previous
    behaviour, never to a failed search.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 02:30:00 +0000

bibliograph (0.1.9) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Give each upstream its own breaker again. `fetchJson` — the shared HTTP
    helper in `api/open-library.ts` — guarded and recorded every request against
    the **OpenLibrary** breaker, whatever host it was calling, and labelled the
    metric `openlibrary` in both directions. The contributor-image resolver uses
    it for Wikidata and Commons too, so an OpenLibrary outage tripped lookups it
    had nothing to do with, and one provider's failures closed the others out.
    It now takes the breaker, metric label and log stage from the caller, and
    the resolver passes `wikidataBreaker` / `commonsBreaker` for its own calls.
  * Stop the description backfills recording a negative they never asked for.
    `resolveDescription` / `resolveWorkDescription` return `undefined` when a
    breaker is open or a request fails, exactly as they do when a book genuinely
    has no description, and the callers stamped `description_checked_at`. Both
    now record the stamp only when every upstream they would consult was
    reachable; otherwise the row stays unchecked and a later run retries it.
    Found in production: twelve editions were stamped while the OpenLibrary
    breaker was open from an unrelated backfill, and those stamps are being
    cleared.
  * Remove the duplicate breaker accounting the resolver did around `fetchJson`
    (each miss was counted twice, tripping a five-failure threshold in three).

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 01:35:00 +0000

bibliograph (0.1.8) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Never record a deferred lookup as a permanent miss. Every backfilled
    entity carries a "checked, nothing found" stamp (`image_checked_at`,
    `description_checked_at`, `bio_checked_at`) so a rerun does not re-ask
    upstream. The contributor-image resolver returned `undefined` both when an
    author genuinely has no portrait and when its circuit breaker was open or
    the request failed — and the backfill stamped both. A bulk run on the host
    opened the OpenLibrary breaker after five consecutive failures, then
    stamped 1,631 contributors as having no photo without asking upstream at
    all; those rows would never have been retried.
  * The image resolver now raises `UpstreamDeferred` for the breaker-open and
    request-failed cases (OpenLibrary author doc, Wikidata search and P18,
    Commons extmetadata). `backfillContributorImageForUri` catches it, warns,
    and leaves the row unchecked, so a later run retries it. The resolver also
    gained a named `ContributorImageResolver` contract and an injectable
    parameter, which is what the new regression test drives: deferral writes no
    stamp, an authoritative miss does, and a checked row is skipped.
  * Add `--limit` to `backfill-contributor-images`. A first fill of thousands
    of authors fans out to three upstreams, so it is safer enqueued in bounded
    batches with pauses between them, which the flag makes possible.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 01:25:00 +0000

bibliograph (0.1.7) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Render images on `/search`. The results list branched on `$type` to pick a
    thumbnail, but the loader returned the search-source items verbatim and no
    source carries one, so no row ever painted an image. The loader now stamps
    `$type` per kind and each row shows a fixed-size thumbnail: the edition
    cover, or the contributor portrait from the local `contributors.image_url`
    cache (filled by `backfill-contributor-images`). Covers win over nothing and
    OL-derived items cannot erase a cached portrait during the remote/local
    merge, because they carry no `imageUrl` key at all.
  * Bound every backfill scan. `backfill-descriptions` and
    `backfill-contributor-images` re-read the same `LIMIT n` page until the
    worker drained it, so with no worker running they never terminated. Both now
    page by primary key with a keyset cursor: each row is enqueued exactly once
    per run and termination no longer depends on a worker mutating rows while
    the scan is in flight. Rows added after the cursor has passed are picked up
    by the next run.
  * Stop re-arming queued work. Every enqueue script now excludes rows that
    already have a job waiting (`NOT EXISTS graphile_worker.jobs`), and the
    description/contributor-image scans also skip their permanent-miss stamps.
    A rerun is cheap and reports `0`, instead of re-arming every pending job.
  * Add a progress bar to the seven backfill CLIs (`scripts/lib/progress.ts`):
    one line on stderr with rate and ETA, degraded to periodic lines when
    stderr is not a TTY so cron logs stay readable. stdout keeps its JSON
    summary. `runBackfill` gained an optional `onProgress(phase, done, total)`
    hook, sized lazily so callers that do not listen still see one query per
    page.
  * Add the record and admin surfaces from the working tree: edition merge,
    edition votes with a librarian-request path, a shared record hero/body,
    catalog delete, and the contributor-bio and work-description backfills with
    their `checked_at` bookkeeping (migration 0013).
  * Run the search task list under `mise run dev`. `mise run dev` started the
    TAP consumer and the web server but not `src/worker-search-jobs.ts`, the
    only process that drains the `ingest-*` and `backfill-*` queues, so those
    jobs piled up untouched on a developer machine.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Mon, 02 Nov 2026 01:10:00 +0000

bibliograph (0.1.6) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Attach Wikidata identity links to editions, works and contributors. A new
    enricher resolves each item's Open Library ID through Wikidata's SPARQL
    endpoint (`P648`) and appends what it finds to the record's existing
    `identifiers` array: the item's own `wikidata` Q-id, plus `viaf` and
    `isni` when it carries `P214`/`P213`. The links belong there rather than in
    new top-level fields because every XRPC handler hand-maps its response, so
    a `wikidataUri` property would be dropped at the boundary and never
    persisted; `identifiers` already declares all three resources in its
    lexicon `knownValues`.
  * Join on the Open Library ID, not the ISBN. `P212` (ISBN-13) was measured at
    20.7s per query and returned nothing for real catalog ISBNs, while `P648`
    is indexed and answers in about a second.
  * Bind by identifier only, never by name. Several Wikidata items claim the
    same OLID, so the highest sitelink count wins and the lowest Q-number
    breaks ties. An exact-label match on a contributor name would bind
    whichever homonym Wikidata ranks highest, and sync ingest persists the
    result, so name-only contributors pass through untouched.
  * Keep the lookup off the critical path: a match is cached for a day and a
    miss for an hour, queries are chunked 50 identifiers at a time and spaced
    a second apart, and an open breaker, a 429 or a failing endpoint degrades
    to the unenriched items instead of failing the search.

 -- Bibliograph Maintainers <maintainers@olamaelcu.net>  Sun, 01 Nov 2026 21:40:00 +0000

bibliograph (0.1.5) unstable; urgency=medium

  * Keep the ISBNs an OpenLibrary edition document carries. The direct edition
    mapper read the cover, year, language and contributors but dropped
    `isbn_13`/`isbn_10`, so every `ol.*` record was indexed with no ISBN at
    all — 1173 of the 1186 ISBN-less catalog editions — even though
    OpenLibrary publishes them.
  * Add an ISBN backfill job. It resolves the authority an edition was derived
    from (OpenLibrary edition, Google Books volume, or the ISBNdb rkey) and
    appends the ISBNs that authority reports, recomputing the record CID. When
    the authority has none it searches OpenLibrary and Google Books, prunes the
    candidates on title, author surname, year and page count, and records what
    it found in `edition_isbn_enrichment` without writing identifiers — the
    fuzzy tier runs in shadow mode because a wrong ISBN repoints a reader's
    shelf at a different physical book.
  * Run the backfill from `bibliograph-isbn-backfill.timer` every 15 minutes.
    Each tick enqueues one bounded batch of 200 rows for the existing jobs
    service; the bookkeeping table makes the scan finite, so a tick with
    nothing left to do costs one indexed query.

